window.docContents[44].resolve({"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Hash-Maps--Creation":{"contents":"Creates a new empty hash map. The optional parameter capacity can be supplied to presize the\nmap so that it can hold the given number of mappings without reallocating. It is also possible to\nuse the empty collection notations ∅ and {} to create an empty hash map with the default\ncapacity.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Basic Types\u0009Maps and Sets\u0009Hash Maps","header":"21.19.2.1. Creation","id":"/Basic-Types/Maps-and-Sets/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Basic-Types--Maps-and-Sets--Hash-Maps--Creation"},"/Iterators/Reasoning-About-Iterators/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Iterators--Reasoning-About-Iterators--Monads-for-Reasoning":{"contents":"PostconditionT m α represents an operation in the monad m together with a\nintrinsic proof that some postcondition holds for the α valued monadic result.\nIt consists of a predicate P about α and an element of m ({ a // P a }) and is a helpful tool\nfor intrinsic verification, notably termination proofs, in the context of iterators.PostconditionT m is a monad if m is. However, note that PostconditionT m α is a structure,\nso that the compiler will generate inefficient code from recursive functions returning\nPostconditionT m α. Optimizations for ReaderT, StateT etc. aren't applicable for structures.Moreover, PostconditionT m α is not a well-behaved monad transformer because PostconditionT.lift\nneither commutes with pure nor with bind.A predicate that holds for the return value(s) of the m-monadic operation.The actual monadic operation. Its return value is bundled together with a proof that\nit satisfies Property.\n\nConverts an operation from PostConditionT m to m, discarding the postcondition.\n\nLifts an operation from m to PostconditionT m without asserting any nontrivial postcondition.Caution: lift is not a lawful lift function.\nFor example, pure a : PostconditionT m α is not the same as\nPostconditionT.lift (pure a : m α).\n\nLifts a monadic value from m { a : α // P a } to a value PostconditionT m α.\n\nAsserts that a certain iterator it could plausibly yield the value out after an arbitrary\nnumber of steps.The output value could plausibly be emitted in the next step.The output value could plausibly be emitted in a step after the next step.\n\nIf m is a monad, then HetT m is a monad that has two features:* It generalizes m to arbitrary universes.* It tracks a postcondition property that holds for the monadic return value, similarly to\nPostconditionT.This monad is noncomputable and is merely a vehicle for more convenient proofs, especially proofs\nabout the equivalence of iterators, because it avoids universe issues and spares users the work\nto handle the postconditions manually.Caution: Just like PostconditionT, this is not a lawful monad transformer.\nTo lift from m to HetT m, use HetT.lift.Because this monad is fundamentally universe-polymorphic, it is recommended for consistency to\nalways use the methods HetT.pure, HetT.map and HetT.bind instead of the homogeneous versions\nPure.pure, Functor.map and Bind.bind.A predicate that holds for the return value(s) of the m-monadic operation.A proof that the possible return values are equivalent to a w-small type.The actual monadic operation. Its return value is bundled together with a proof that\nit satisfies Property and squashed so that it fits into the monad m.\n\nA noncomputable variant of IterM.step using the HetT monad.\nIt is used in the definition of the equivalence relations on iterators,\nnamely IterM.Equiv and Iter.Equiv.\n\nLifts x : m α into HetT m α with the trivial postcondition.Caution: This is not a lawful monad lifting function\n\nApplies the given function to the result of the contained m-monadic operation with a\nproof that the postcondition property holds, returning another operation in m.\n\nA universe-heterogeneous version of Pure.pure. Given a : α, it returns an element of HetT m α\nwith the postcondition (a = ·).\n\nA universe-heterogeneous version of Functor.map.\n\nA generalization of HetT.map that provides the postcondition property to the mapping function.\n\nA universe-heterogeneous version of Bind.bind.\n\nA generalization of HetT.bind that provides the postcondition property to the mapping function.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Iterators\u0009Reasoning About Iterators","header":"23.5.3. Monads for Reasoning","id":"/Iterators/Reasoning-About-Iterators/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Iterators--Reasoning-About-Iterators--Monads-for-Reasoning"},"/releases/v4.17.0/#release-v4___17___0":{"contents":"For this release, 319 changes landed. In addition to the 168 feature additions and 57 fixes listed below there were 12 refactoring changes, 13 documentation improvements and 56 chores.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes","header":"Lean 4.17.0 (2025-03-03)","id":"/releases/v4.17.0/#release-v4___17___0"},"/releases/v4.20.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___20___0-_LPAR_2025-06-02_RPAR_--Highlights":{"contents":"The Lean v4.20.0 release brings multiple new features, bug fixes, improvements to Lake, and groundwork for the module system.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.20.0 (2025-06-02)","header":"Highlights","id":"/releases/v4.20.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___20___0-_LPAR_2025-06-02_RPAR_--Highlights"},"/releases/v4.22.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___22___0-_LPAR_2025-08-14_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #8702 enhances the PR release workflow to create both short format and\nSHA-suffixed release tags. Creates both pr-release-{PR_NUMBER} and\npr-release-{PR_NUMBER}-{SHORT_SHA} tags, generates separate releases for\nboth formats, adds separate GitHub status checks, and updates\nBatteries/Mathlib testing branches to use SHA-suffixed tags for exact\ncommit traceability.* #8710 pins the precise hash of softprops/action-gh-release to* #9033 adds a Mathlib-like testing and feedback system for the\nreference manual. Lean PRs will receive comments that reflect the status\nof the language reference with respect to the PR.* #9092 further updates release automation. The per-repository update\nscripts script/release_steps.py now actually performs the tests,\nrather than outputting a script for the release manager to run line by\nline. It's been tested on v4.21.0 (i.e. the easy case of a stable\nrelease), and we'll debug its behaviour on v4.22.0-rc1 tonight.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.22.0 (2025-08-14)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.22.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___22___0-_LPAR_2025-08-14_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.25.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___25___0-_LPAR_2025-11-14_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #10383 includes some improvements to the release process, making the\nupdating of stable branches more robust, and including cslib in the\nrelease checklist.* #10389 fixes a bug where string literal parsing ignored its trailing\nwhitespace setting.* #10460 introduces a simple script that adjusts module headers in a\npackage for use of the module system, without further minimizing import\nor annotation use.* #10476 fixes the dead let elimination code in the kernel's\ninfer_let function.* #10575 adds the necessary infrastructure for recording elaboration\ndependencies that may not be apparent from the resulting environment\nsuch as notations and other metaprograms. An adapted version of shake\nfrom Mathlib is added to script/ but may be moved to another location\nor repo in the future.* #10777 improves the scripts assisting with cutting Lean releases (by\nreporting CI status of open PRs, and adding documentation), and adds a\n.claude/commands/release.md prompt file so Claude can assist.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.25.0 (2025-11-14)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.25.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___25___0-_LPAR_2025-11-14_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.25.1/#release-v4___25___1":{"contents":"Lean v4.25.1 is a bug fix release fixing a caching problem in lake, affecting certain downstream dependencies of ProofWidgets4.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes","header":"Lean 4.25.1 (2025-11-18)","id":"/releases/v4.25.1/#release-v4___25___1"},"/releases/v4.26.0/#release-v4___26___0":{"contents":"For this release, 264 changes landed. In addition to the 84 feature additions and 73 fixes listed below there were 10 refactoring changes, 7 documentation improvements, 13 performance improvements, 8 improvements to the test suite and 69 other changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes","header":"Lean 4.26.0 (2025-12-13)","id":"/releases/v4.26.0/#release-v4___26___0"},"/releases/v4.34.1/#release-v4___34___1":{"contents":"This patch release contains multiple runtime fixes,\nand we encourage all users of v4.34.0 or earlier to upgrade to it.\n\nFor this release, 3 changes landed.\nIn addition to the 0 feature additions\nand 3 fixes listed below,\nthere were 0 refactoring changes,\n0 documentation improvements,\n0 performance improvements,\n0 improvements to the test suite,\nand 0 other changes.\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes","header":"Lean 4.34.1 (2026-09-24)","id":"/releases/v4.34.1/#release-v4___34___1"},"/releases/v4.35.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___35___0-_LPAR_2026-10-12_RPAR_--Other":{"contents":"* #15542\nmakes the LeanExport NDJSON reader used by leanchecker --from-export and lake check reject malformed input that it previously accepted silently: bytes after the JSON object on a line, JSON objects with duplicate keys, and lines that bind a name, level, expression or recursor rule index that is already bound (including the predefined index 0 for the anonymous name and level zero).* #15130\nbundles the con-leche external checker with release toolchains, so it can be used as an independent checker without a separate install.* #15099\nbundles the nanoda external checker with release toolchains, so it can be used as an independent checker without a separate install.* #15048\nbundles the lean4lean external checker with release toolchains, so it can be used as an independent checker without a separate install.* #15055\nruns the default kernel in a privilege separated process in lake check and lake challenge. This does not fix any concrete security issues but merely serves as an additional hardening measure.* #14884\nadds bin/leanchecker-paranoid, a variant of leanchecker built with allocator hardening.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.35.0 (2026-10-12)","header":"Other","id":"/releases/v4.35.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___35___0-_LPAR_2026-10-12_RPAR_--Other"},"/releases/v4.35.0/#release-v4___35___0":{"contents":"For this release, 214 changes landed.\nIn addition to the 78 feature additions\nand 57 fixes listed below,\nthere were 18 refactoring changes,\n7 documentation improvements,\n23 performance improvements,\n2 improvements to the test suite,\nand 29 other changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes","header":"Lean 4.35.0 (2026-10-12)","id":"/releases/v4.35.0/#release-v4___35___0"},"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-10-14_RPAR_--Tactics":{"contents":"* #15678\nfixes grind failing to prove goals such as c = b → a ≤ b + c + d → a ≤ b + b + d in an ordered ring when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). The normalizer rewrites b + b + d to 2 * b + d, so the two inequalities are no longer congruent after c = b, and no module related the two sums.* #15675\nfixes grind failing with the internal error \"structure does not have a lawful LT instance\" (or \"structure does not support LT\") when linarith has to reason about < on a type without a LawfulOrderLT instance. For example, [IntModule α] [LE α] [LT α] [IsLinearOrder α] [OrderedAdd α] (a b c : α) : a < b → b < c → a < c now fails with a regular grind failed message.* #15672\nfixes grind failing to use k⁻¹ for a numeral k in a field when the Sym.simp normalizer is enabled (set_option backward.grind.normalizer false). For example, [Field R] [IsCharP R 0] (h : f (a * 2⁻¹) = 1) (h2 : a = 2 * b) : f b = 1 was not proved.* #15642\nadds the option Grind.Config.distrib (default false) to the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer. By default, a product of two non-constant polynomials such as x * (y + 1), a power of a sum, and the inverse of a product are kept as atoms whose factors are normalized, as the legacy normalizer does; grind +distrib expands them into polynomial normal form. Linear arithmetic procedures treat nonlinear terms as atoms, so keeping them unexpanded preserves facts such as x * (y + 1) = x * z after y + 1 = z is derived.* #15624\nmakes grind put k#w literals with a symbolic width w in the OfNat.ofNat form it uses for BitVec literals, so with the Sym.simp-based normalizer theorems such as BitVec.sdiv_zero apply to x.sdiv 0 as they do with the legacy normalizer.* #15623\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite dependent propositional binder domains, so ∀ h : i < a.size, q h becomes ∀ h : i + 1 ≤ a.size, ... as with the legacy normalizer.* #15619\nfixes a panic in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer on match equations whose patterns contain arithmetic, e.g. grind [h.eq_def] for a function matching on Vec α (n + 1 + 1).* #15618\nfixes spurious ring term with unexpected instance issues in grind for casts over abstract rings, e.g. (m + n) • a = m • a + n • a with [CommRing R], and the corresponding failures of the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15616\nadds BitVec.zero, BitVec.ofNat _ (Bool.toNat _), and BitVec.carry to the list of supported terms by bv_decide* #15612\nfixes grind losing a hypothesis whose type is marked [grind cases eager] when the hypothesis is introduced by intros or asserted as a parameter fact. Only the constructor fields survived in each branch, so example : P → ¬P → False := by grind and grind [(mkP)] on the goal P failed for a predicate P with field-less constructors.* #15611\nfixes grind? failing with \"proof contains unresolved internal metavariable\" when a parameter fact has a type marked [grind cases eager]. Asserting such a fact splits the goal during initialization, and grind? only produced a script for the first goal. It now handles every goal, and the suggested script carries one · block per goal.* #15609\nmakes grind [mkP] accept a theorem whose statement is not a ∀ and is not indexable, e.g., theorem mkP : P for a nullary inductive predicate P. The parameter is asserted as a fact, so the workaround grind [(mkP)] is no longer needed. grind? and finish? keep such identifiers in their suggestions.* #15606\nfixes a spurious \"generated tactic cannot close the goal\" warning from finish? when its parameters alone close the goal, e.g. grind => finish? [inj f_inj].* #15602\nfixes grind? and finish? dropping parameters such as inj f_inj and funCC q from their suggestions, which then failed when pasted.* #15600\nfixes grind failing on instances wrapped in @[implicit_reducible] definitions.* #15599\nfixes a grind internal error, ring term has not been internalized, triggered when the ring solver learned a disequality such as a ≠ b while a term containing a - b was still being internalized. The Rabinowitsch step then built (a - b) * (a - b)⁻¹ and internalized it before (a - b)⁻¹ had an ENode. Closes #15590.* #15589\nfixes a panic in grind ring when propagating implied equalities in rings without NoNatZeroDivisors, such as BitVec n.* #15588\nfixes a grind internal error (ring term has not been internalized) triggered when a user-defined Pow Int Nat instance with higher priority is in scope. Mathlib's Monoid.npow instance on Int hits this case after #15506.* #15555\nmakes vcgen take the program type and the WP instance from the goal's wp application when it applies a spec. Thus vcgen works on a goal whose program is not of the instance's program type, for example a program e : Exp in a goal wp (Prog := Tagged n Exp) e Q E for def Tagged (n : Nat) (α : Type) := α.* #15582\nmakes cbv evaluate cond (bif c then a else b) lazily, like ite and dite. Before this change, cbv evaluated both branches of cond, even when the condition was the literal true. An expensive or non-terminating branch that the condition does not select cost time or exhausted cbv.maxSteps. The fix gives the cond simproc pattern all four arguments of cond.* #15497\nadds a guard to WP.Frames, so that vcgen can frame a resource across a program whose footprint depends on the state.* #15526\nfixes a kernel error when sym => simp rewrites inside a non-dependent arrow (e.g. x + 0 = y → p) in a file using module.* #15515\nmakes vcgen split a spec precondition fun _ => ∃ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] into the VC i < xs.size and the entailment for Q () xs[i] under a hypothesis h, so that the wp of the program tail in the body is stepped. The precondition fun _ => ⨆ h : i < xs.size, Q () xs[i] splits the same way.* #15524\nfixes a grind internal error (unknown free variable) on hints such as grind [h _ _ pf] whose only hole is a proof. It broke List.pairwise_iff_forall_infix in Mathlib after #15506.* #15506\nfixes a grind internal error (semiring term has not been internalized) triggered when power propagation rebuilds a term whose arguments are still being internalized (#15479).* #15503\nfixes sym with arith and grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer on goals whose equations are over a type in Sort u, such as {α : Sort u} (a b : α). The ring classifier computed the universe level of the carrier with getDecLevel, which fails with \"invalid universe level, u is not greater than 0\" for such types; it now uses getDecLevel? and reports that the type has no ring structure, as the order classifier already did.* #15491\nmakes vcgen recognize more @[spec] preconditions as conjunctive in the schematic postcondition Q/E. vcgen applies such a spec directly and runs no frame inference for it.* #15487\nmakes vcgen split a pointwise ∧ in a spec's precondition like a lattice meet ⊓.* #15483\nmakes Sym.simp rewrite a raw Nat literal that is not the argument of a numeral into the OfNat.ofNat form, as Meta.simp does. Rewrite rules whose pattern variable binds the raw literal of a numeral, such as the [grind hom] rule (OfNat.ofNat a : Fin n).val = a % n, produced terms like nat_lit 0 % (p + 1) that no later step recognized as a numeral, so grind with the Sym.simp-based normalizer failed on goals over Fin (p + 1) and BitVec n.* #15482\nmakes grind prove goals over UIntN and BitVec that multiply by a numeral close to the modulus, such as -1 * a or 4294967295 * a over UInt32; these previously exhausted the lia step budget. The [grind hom] injection into Nat turned (c * a).toNat into c * a.toNat % 2^w, and cutsat splits on the coefficient c when it has to eliminate a.toNat exactly, while (-a).toNat is (2^w - a.toNat) % 2^w, with coefficient 1.* #15478\nfixes a kernel-rejected proof in grind's ring module. For an inverse a⁻¹ with a numeral a, processInv asserts a * a⁻¹ = 1 or a⁻¹ = 0 with theorems whose numeral is denoteInt k, i.e. an OfNat numeral or its negation, but the recognizer also accepted ↑(0 : Nat), ↑(3 : Int), -0, and - -3, which are only propositionally equal to denoteInt k; Field.inv_zero : 0⁻¹ = 0 was then pushed as a proof of (↑0)⁻¹ = ↑0.* #15476\nmakes the Sym arithmetic normalizer rewrite a cast of a numeral (↑(0 : Nat), ↑(-2 : Int)) to the numeral of the carrier wherever it occurs, as the legacy grind normalizer does. Casts were interpreted only inside +, *, ...; a cast standing alone, as a function argument or under ⁻¹, was kept, so (↑0)⁻¹ reached the ring solver of grind, which applied Field.inv_zero to it and produced a proof rejected by the kernel (grind_9321 with backward.grind.normalizer := false).* #15475\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer rewrite every Fin literal to its canonical form. A literal such as @OfNat.ofNat (Fin (1 + 1)) 0 Fin.instOfNat, with a nested numeral as value and a ground type index, was left as is, and grind treated it as a literal distinct from (0 : Fin 2), producing proofs rejected by the kernel (grind_canon_ofnat).* #15473\nadds a Sym.dsimp pass after Sym.simp in the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, so that beta-redexes and projections of structure instances in the fixed arguments of an application are reduced, as the legacy normalizer does with Simp.Config.dsimp. Sym.simp does not visit these arguments, and a redex left in the implicit objects of a morphism composition made the goals of Functor.flip in grind_cat.lean miss NatTrans.naturality.* #15467\nmakes the projection reduction of Sym (Sym.reduceProjApp?, used by Sym.simp, Sym.dsimp, and the canonicalizer) expose the structure argument at .reducible transparency, as simp does, instead of the ambient one. With the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer, E-matching patterns are normalized at attribute time at .default transparency, so a pattern such as (asIso f).hom unfolded asIso and collapsed to a variable, and the theorem was rejected with invalid pattern.* #15466\nfixes two ways a grind congruence table entry could go stale, which made grind.debug fail nondeterministically on goals with match conditions, and adds a deterministic invariant check for the table.* #15465\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer respect the annotations on match equations: Grind.simpMatchDiscrsOnly (only the discriminants of the match are simplified) and Grind.PreMatchCond (the condition is left as is). E-matching instances of match equations, e.g. from f.eq_def, now work with backward.grind.normalizer := false, where they used to fail or produce proofs rejected by the kernel.* #15464\nadds the option backward.grind.normalizer. It defaults to true, which selects the legacy simp-based grind normalizer; with false, grind normalizes hypotheses, the target, E-matching instances, generalized patterns, and E-matching patterns at attribute time with the Sym.simp-based normalizer developed with grind_norm. The default behavior is unchanged.* #15463\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Int.toNat, Int.natAbs, and toNat on UInt8, UInt16, UInt32, and UInt64 literals, e.g. (-7 : Int).natAbs becomes 7. grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15461\nmakes the realized constants required by bv_decide private to avoid name clashes while\nimporting multiple modules that realized them.* #15460\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate String.push and String.singleton on literals, e.g. \"abc\".push 'd' becomes \"abcd\". grind is affected too, since the [grind hom] rewriter uses evalGround.* #15458\nmakes the Sym.simp-based grind normalizer produce bit-vector literals in OfNat.ofNat form, the form grind keeps, instead of BitVec.ofNat. Ground bit-vector terms such as 3#8 + 5#8, 3 &&& 5, (3#8).zeroExtend 16, and 300#8 are now evaluated to the same literals as the legacy normalizer.* #15448\nfixes a kernel error in grind on goals mentioning both Char.ofNat 97 and 'a', and makes grind evaluate Char.toNat, Char.val, and Char.ofNat when the argument is equal to a literal, e.g. y = 'a' → y.toNat = 97 and n = 97 → Char.ofNat n = 'a'.* #15445\nmakes grind prove goals about Fin.log2 and about the scoped Nat and Int casts into Fin n through their val interpretation, such as (x.log2).val = Nat.log2 x.val, ((i : Int) : Fin n).val = (i % n).toNat, and ((k : Nat) : Fin n).val = k % n.* #15434\nmakes the Sym.Arith normalizer solve equations over commutative rings with a nonzero characteristic, such as Fin n, UInt8, and BitVec w, when it runs in the mode used by the grind normalizer. An equation k * m + k' = 0 or k * m₁ = k * m₂ whose coefficient k is invertible modulo the characteristic is solved for the monomial: in Fin 5, 4 * x + 2 = 0 becomes x = 2, as 2 * x = 6 already becomes x = 3 over Int.* #15433\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate the Fin functions on literals: succ, castSucc, rev, last, val, pred, castAdd, addNat, natAdd, castLT, castLE, subNat, mk, and ofNat. It also normalizes Fin literals: (7 : Fin 5) becomes 2, and a type index that is not a numeral, as in (4 : Fin (5 + 1)), is evaluated. Fin arithmetic and comparisons were already supported.* #15398\nintroduces a CEGAR/Lemmas on Demand procedure into bv_decide for supporting uninterpreted functions even without interacting with grind mode. Towards this it refactors the solving pipeline to be fully incremental and makes use of incremental SAT solving. The congruence feature is available behind bv_decide +uf, it is currently limited to:1. Only functions that return Bool/BitVec2. Only performing congruence reasoning on non-dependent Bool/BitVec arguments (see the bv_uf test for examples of what this entails)* #15420\nfixes a grind regression introduced by #15378: a goal such as Imp (∀ i, i < n → ∃ j, j = i + 1 ∧ p j) (p 4), where Imp a b unfolds to a → b through a [grind norm] rule, was no longer proved, while the same goal written with a syntactic → still was. grind now normalizes the goal before negating it, and the normalizer no longer rewrites (∀ x, p x) → q into (∃ x, ¬p x) ∨ q.* #15419\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char.val on character literals, e.g. 'a'.val becomes 97. It complements #15411, which added the other Char operations.* #15417\nfixes an Application type mismatch error in grind when the homomorphism rewriter translates an equality whose hypothesis mentions a let-bound literal, e.g. b = m.cpop with let m : BitVec 2 := 1#2. Closes #15388.* #15411\nextends Sym.Simp.evalGround to evaluate Char operations on character literals: toNat, toLower, toUpper, toString, isWhitespace, isUpper, isLower, isAlpha, isDigit, isAlphanum, ==, and !=. It also converts Char.ofNat n into a character literal when n is a numeral, so that a character has a single representation.* #15409\nadds Sym.Simp.reduceControl, a variant of simpControl that does not block the simplifier: it simplifies the condition of ite/dite/cond and the discriminants of match, reduces the term when they are decided, and otherwise lets the simplifier visit the branches. The Sym.simp-based grind normalizer uses it in place of reduceMatcher.* #15405\nfixes a sharing violation in the arith simproc of Sym.simp, e.g., on a + f (b + 0). With sym.debug enabled, it surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15322\nmakes Sym.intros introduce a binder whose name starts with __ as an implementation-detail local, as the elaborator does for such binders. vcgen introduces the join point __do_jp of a do block with Sym.intros, so its verification conditions no longer show the join point and its body in the local context.* #15389\nfixes a sharing violation in Sym.dsimp when it descends into a fun, ∀, or let binder whose bound variable occurs in the body or in a later binder type. With sym.debug enabled, the violation surfaced as a panic in assertShared.* #15383\nadds the fallback modifier to the [grind hom] attribute. A fallback rule is applied to a term only when no other homomorphism rule matches it, using the second tier Sym.simp already has for eq_def unfolding.* #15378\nfixes the pushNot simproc of the grind normalizer so that ¬(p → q) becomes p ∧ ¬q and ¬∀ x, p x becomes ∃ x, ¬p x. These rewrites have been dead code since #9271: the simproc matched the forallE case against the whole ¬p instead of p, and checked the domain with the syntactic Expr.isProp, which is false for a proposition variable. The same isProp check is fixed in the Sym.simp-based normalizer.* #15377\nremoves the grind = attribute from the lemmas that are already [grind hom] rules, so that grind derives the toNat, toInt and val image of a term once, through the homomorphism rewriter, instead of twice.* #15370\nmakes grind prove goals that mention the Bool-valued signed comparisons BitVec.slt and BitVec.sle, such as (x < y) ↔ (x.toBitVec.slt y.toBitVec = true) for Int64, x.slt y = true ↔ x.toInt < y.toInt, x.slt y → y.sle z → x.slt z and x.slt y = !(y.sle x).* #15369\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bitwise complement, such as (~~~x).toInt = (~~~x.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16) for BitVec 16 and the corresponding goals for the fixed-width signed integer types, as well as plain Int facts such as ~~~a = -a - 1.* #15367\nmakes grind prove goals about the signed value of a bit vector shifted left by a variable amount, such as (x <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt <<< n).bmod (2 ^ 16), and lets nested signed results compose, such as ((x <<< m) <<< n).toInt = (x.toInt * 2 ^ m * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16), ((x + y) <<< n).toInt = ((x.toInt + y.toInt) * 2 ^ n).bmod (2 ^ 16) and ((x * y) * z).toInt = (x.toInt * y.toInt * z.toInt).bmod (2 ^ 16).* #15366\nmakes simp [f] and dsimp [f] in sym => mode behave like the default simplifier when f is a definition or a conditional theorem. Side conditions of conditional theorems, such as the equational theorems of definitions with overlapping patterns, are now discharged using grind. Non-recursive definitions are unfolded when no equational theorem applies. dsimp accepts definitions and rfl-theorems. Reducible definitions are ignored with a warning instead of failing.* #15363\nfixes a grind failure on goals containing -0 : Int, such as f (-((0 : Nat) : Int)) = f 0, where grind produced a proof rejected by the kernel. -0 is now normalized to 0.* #15365\nmakes grind relate the unsigned value of an arithmetic shift to the signed value of its operand: goals such as ((x.sshiftRight n).toNat : Int) = (x.toInt / 2 ^ n) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15364\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector rotations through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x.rotateLeft n).toNat = (x.toNat * 2 ^ (n % 16) + x.toNat / 2 ^ (16 - n % 16)) % 2 ^ 16 over BitVec 16, and the rotateRight counterpart, are now proved.* #15362\nfixes Sym.DSimp.zetaDelta and Sym.DSimp.zetaDeltaAll so that they unfold a let-bound variable that appears as the head of an application. Previously, dsimp [foo] and dsimp [*] in grind/sym, and the bv_decide normalization passes, left terms such as foo a b untouched when foo := fun x y => x + y was a local definition, while Meta.DSimp reduces them to a + b.* #15350\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector exponentiation through its [grind hom] injections: goals such as (x.pow z).toNat = x.toNat ^ z % 2 ^ 16 and (x.pow z).toInt = (x.toInt ^ z).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 are now proved.* #15348\nmakes grind reason about bit-vector concatenation through its [grind hom] injection into Nat: goals such as (x ++ y).toNat = x.toNat * 2 ^ 8 + y.toNat over BitVec 8 are now proved. It also fixes Sym.simp rules whose left-hand side has Nat arithmetic in a type index, such as the width m + n of (x ++ y).toNat, which never fired on terms whose width had been normalized to a numeral.* #15347\nlets the cutsat solver in grind reorder its variables more than once during a proof search. Variables created after the first model search, e.g. by later E-matching rounds, were appended to the variable order; when such a variable has a large coefficient in its bound constraints, the Cooper case split that eliminates it enumerated that coefficient and exhausted liaSteps. For example, (x <<< 1).toInt = (x.toInt <<< 1).bmod (2 ^ 16) over BitVec 16 failed because the quotient x.toNat / 2 ^ 16, introduced by the instance of BitVec.toInt_eq_toNat_bmod, ended up last in the order with a coefficient of 2 ^ 16. Such goals are now proved.* #15345\nadds support for &&& with a constant mask to grind, for masks of the form 1…10…0 (a run of ones followed by a run of zeros), over Nat and, through the [grind hom] injections, BitVec and the fixed-width integer types. The mask is rewritten into %, /, and * by literals, which cutsat supports: x &&& (2^n - 1) becomes x % 2^n, and x &&& ((2^n - 1) * 2^k) becomes x / 2^k % 2^n * 2^k. For example, (x &&& 0xe0).toNat = x.toNat / 32 % 8 * 32 over BitVec 8 and x &&& 0xC0 = 0 → x &&& 0x40 = 0 over UInt8 are now proved by grind. Masks with holes (e.g. 0b101) and Int are not handled yet.* #15344\nmakes Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) cancel x * x⁻¹ for an atom x when a discharger proves x ≠ 0, in any field. a * b / a becomes b, a ^ 3 / a becomes a ^ 2, and a / (2 * a) becomes 2⁻¹. The simproc DSL accepts arith with d, so arith with grind uses the goal's hypotheses. A result that asked for a side condition is context-dependent.* #15343\nmakes numeral inverses rational coefficients in Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence in the arith simproc of Sym.simp) for fields of characteristic zero. a / 2 + a / 2 now normalizes to a, a / 2 + b / 3 to (3 * a + 2 * b) * 6⁻¹, and relations become denominator-free: a / 2 + b / 3 = 1 becomes 3 * a + 2 * b = 6. Without an IsCharP _ 0 instance, numeral inverses remain atoms.* #15259\nregisters BitVec.toNat_ofInt as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the unsigned value of BitVec.ofInt. In particular, cutsat can now follow carries computed through BitVec.ofInt.* #15262\nregisters BitVec.toInt_signExtend as a [grind hom] rule, so grind can reason about the signed value of BitVec.signExtend: narrowing reduces toInt modulo 2 ^ v, and widening leaves it unchanged.* #15311\nfixes grind never activating E-matching theorems whose ground pattern contains a lambda, a dependent ∀, or a nonparametric literal such as a Char or a negative Int. For example, @[grind .] theorem myPred_of_true : myPred (fun _ => True) was never used even when the goal contained myPred mySet and mySet = fun _ => True, and @[grind =] theorem fc_a : fc 'a' = 1 could not prove fc 'a' = 1. Passing such theorems explicitly with grind [myPred_of_true] worked, since explicit parameters bypass symbol indexing.* #15310\nfixes Sym.dsimp producing terms with dangling free variables when a binder type depends on an earlier binder, e.g. ∀ k : Nat, k < 32 → .... This caused bv_decide to fail with unknown free variable. Closes #15195.* #15046\nfixes Lean.Meta.Sym.Simp.toHave, which passed the dependencies of a have to Expr.betaRev in forward order even though betaRev expects them reversed. Any have whose value depended on two or more earlier haves of the same telescope was rebuilt with its dependencies swapped, producing an ill-typed proof term that the kernel rejects with an application type mismatch.* #14827\nmakes vcgen state exception postcondition verification conditions pointwise for more exception postcondition types, and makes throws clauses work for custom types. For Except ε, a spec with the exception postcondition fun e => e = \"x\" now yields ∀ e, e = \"x\" → … in place of an entailment between two functions. A custom type such as structure Thrown where onThrow : String → Prop gets the same pointwise verification conditions from a ToEStack instance, which converts it to an exception postcondition stack. With an EPostSlot instance, the type also supports throws clauses of def contracts.* #15290\nmoves the syntax of the vcgen tactic to the new module Std.WP.Tactic and deprecates the tactics of the Std.Do proof mode. import Std.WP now provides vcgen. Each call of a proof mode tactic, such as mintro or mspec, reports one deprecation warning that points to vcgen. The option mvcgen.warning is deprecated because it has no effect.* #15298\nteaches Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) the field rewrites that need no side conditions. In a Field, a / b becomes a * b⁻¹, and inverses are pushed down to the atoms: (a * b)⁻¹ ↦ a⁻¹ * b⁻¹, (-a)⁻¹ ↦ -a⁻¹, a⁻¹⁻¹ ↦ a, 0⁻¹ ↦ 0, 1⁻¹ ↦ 1, after normalizing the term under the inverse. x⁻¹ for an atom or numeral x is then an atom of the polynomial, so a / b + a / b normalizes to 2 * (a * b⁻¹).* #15297\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to non-commutative Ring and Semiring structures. Monomials keep the order of their factors, so a * b and b * a stay distinct and (a + b)^2 normalizes to a^2 + a*b + b*a + b^2. Terms, relations, and the characteristic are supported as for the commutative case.* #15296\nfixes the arith simproc of Sym.simp so that reflexive relations between atoms are closed: x ≤ x and x = x now normalize to 0 ≤ 0 and 0 = 0 and are closed by post, like x + 0 ≤ x already was.* #15295\nmakes the relation normalizer of Sym.Arith (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) use the characteristic of the ring, so that 256 * u + v = v on UInt8 normalizes to 0 = 0 and u + 2 = v is recognized as already normal. Previously only terms were reduced modulo the characteristic.* #15293\nextends Sym.Arith.normalize? (and hence the arith simproc of Sym.simp) to relations lhs = rhs, lhs ≤ rhs, and lhs < rhs over a CommRing or CommSemiring. Everything is moved to one side and split by sign, so x + y = z + 2 * x becomes y = x + z, and a ≤ b + a becomes 0 ≤ b; when both sides normalize to the same polynomial, the goal is closed.* #15292\nadds the arith simproc to Sym.simp, which normalizes ring and semiring terms into polynomial normal form using Sym.Arith.normalize?. It is opt-in through register_sym_simp and intended as a pre simproc: it sees the maximal arithmetic subtree first, simplifies its atoms with the full simplifier, and reifies it once. Normal forms are final (done := true) and cached.* #15280\nmakes Expr.toPolyS and Expr.toPolyS_nc handle a zero exponent like Expr.toPoly does, so x ^ 0 in a semiring normalizes to 1 instead of a monomial with exponent 0. The Sym.Arith normalizer now rewrites x ^ 0 : Nat to 1.* #15279\nadds Sym.Arith.normalize?, which rewrites a term of a CommRing or CommSemiring into polynomial normal form with a proof by reflection. It is the normalizer that Sym.simp will use for arithmetic; this PR provides the SymM-level API and its tests, not the simplifier integration.* #15267\nfixes regressions in bv_decide around projection and match reduction.* #15263\nmoves the classification of order structures (LE, IsPreorder, LT, LawfulOrderLT, IsPartialOrder, IsLinearPreorder, OrderedRing) from grind's order module into Sym.Arith, so other Sym components can reuse it. No user-visible change.* #15190\nmakes the grind ring solver use the ring classification, operator caches, reification, and denotation from Sym.Arith, and deletes the copies it kept under Grind/Arith/CommRing. A type is now classified once per SymM run instead of once per goal, so every goal of a grind run and every tactic of a sym block share the result. Code that drives grind programmatically sees the new layout: the per-goal ring state holds solver data only, Result carries the Sym.Arith ring records, and goalToMessageData and evalCheck take them.\n\n","context":"Lean Reference\u0009Release Notes\u0009Lean 4.36.0-rc1 (2026-10-14)","header":"Tactics","id":"/releases/v4.36.0/#The-Lean-Language-Reference--Release-Notes--Lean-4___36___0-rc1-_LPAR_2026-10-14_RPAR_--Tactics"}});